(Abstract) Tj
T*
(We conducted post-hoc mediation and subgroup analyses on data from our 96-participant) Tj
T*
(trial comparing a digital anxiety intervention plus standard care to standard care alone.) Tj
T*
(Participants with higher baseline anxiety severity \(top quartile\) showed greater absolute) Tj
T*
(symptom reductions \(mean -3.4 vs -1.8 in lower-severity group, p = 0.018\). Mediation) Tj
T*
(modeling suggested that approximately 40% of the intervention's effect on anxiety was) Tj
T*
(mediated through increases in reported behavioral engagement and social re-engagement.) Tj
T*
(Participants achieving at least weekly engagement with core application features showed) Tj
T*
(2.3-fold greater likelihood of meeting the study's clinical response criterion. Subgroup) Tj
T*
(analysis by age revealed larger effect sizes in participants under age 50 compared to) Tj
T*
(older adults \(d = 0.68 vs 0.42\). Acceptability ratings were similar across demographic) Tj
T*
(subgroups. These findings highlight that intervention efficacy depends partly on) Tj
T*
(engagement frequency and suggest that tailored engagement strategies may optimize outcomes) Tj
T*
(in older or initially lower-engagement populations.) Tj
